The ''Television Favorites'' compilation DVD included the pilot episode, "All You Need Is Love"; both parts of the fourth-season finale, "Her Honor"; the fifth-season episodes "Death of a Bailiff" and "Who Was That Mashed Man?"; and the sixth-season episode "Fire", which marked the beginning of Harry's relationship with Christine.

Harry Anderson, Markie Post, and Charles Robinson appeared in the ''[[30 Rock]]'' episode, "[[The One with the Cast of Night Court]]". John Larroquette is also mentioned; Harry says he had just spoken to John, which annoys Markie (who has not had recent contact with her absent former co-star) and begins an argument between them that lasts for most of the story.

==Sequel series==
{{Main|Night Court (2023 TV series)}}
In December 2020, NBC announced it was working on a sequel series to ''Night Court''. The show is executive produced by [[Melissa Rauch]] and Winston Rauch, with Dan Rubin writing. Larroquette returned as Fielding, while the show's central character—played by Melissa Rauch<ref>{{cite web |last1=Andreeva |first1=Nellie |date=April 30, 2021 |title='Night Court': EP Melissa Rauch Set To Star With John Larroquette In Sequel At NBC |url=https://deadline.com/2021/04/night-court-melissa-rauch-set-star-john-larroquette-sequel-nbc-pilot-1234748000/ |access-date=April 30, 2021 |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |language=en}}</ref>—is Abby Stone, a judge and the daughter of Harry Stone. The show is produced by [[Warner Bros. Television Studios]] for NBC.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://deadline.com/2020/12/night-court-sequel-john-larraquette-dan-fielding-harry-stone-daughter-melissa-rauch-nbc-reboot-1234657152/ | title = 'Night Court' Sequel In Works At NBC With John Larroquette As Dan Fielding, Harry Stone's Daughter As Focus & Melissa Rauch As EP | first=  Nellie | last = Andreeva | date =December 16, 2020 | access-date = December 16, 2020 | work = [[Deadline Hollywood]] }}</ref> In May 2021, it was announced that NBC had given a pilot order to a sequel series.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2021/05/night-court-reboot-melissa-rauch-john-larroquette-star-nbc-pilot-order-harrys-daughter-dan-1234749310/|title='Night Court' Sequel Starring Melissa Rauch & John Larroquette Gets NBC Pilot Order|last1=Andreeva|first1=Nellie|date=May 3, 2021|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|language=en|access-date=May 3, 2021}}</ref> In June 2021, [[Ana Villafañe]] joined the cast for the pilot, portraying an Assistant [[District Attorney]] and [[Lacretta]] plays a bailiff Donna "Gurgs" Gurganous.<ref>{{cite web |title='Night Court': Ana Villafañe Joins NBC Sequel Pilot|url=https://deadline.com/2021/06/night-court-ana-villafane-nbc-sequel-pilot-1234771650/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Nellie|last=Andreeva|date=June 8, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title='Night Court': Lacretta Joins NBC Sequel Pilot|url=https://deadline.com/2021/06/night-court-lacretta-cast-nbc-sequel-pilot-1234776659/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Nellie|last=Andreeva|date=June 16, 2021|access-date=June 16, 2021}}</ref> In July 2021, [[Kapil Talwalkar]] joined the cast for the pilot, playing a court's clerk Neil.<ref>{{cite web|title='Night Court': Kapil Talwalkar Joins NBC Sequel Pilot|url=https://deadline.com/2021/07/night-court-kapil-talwalkar-nbc-sequel-pilot-zoeys-extraordinary-playlist-1234787801/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Nellie|last=Andreeva|date=July 7, 2021|access-date=July 7, 2021}}</ref> In September 2021, it was announced that NBC had given the production a series order.<ref>{{cite web|title='Night Court' Sequel Starring Melissa Rauch & John Larroquette Lands Series Order At NBC|url=https://deadline.com/2021/09/night-court-sequel-melissa-rauch-john-larroquette-series-order-nbc-1234843950/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Peter|last=White|date=September 24, 2021|access-date=September 24, 2021}}</ref> In February 2023, it was announced that following the strong reception of the first four episodes, NBC had ordered a full second season of the show.<ref>{{cite web|title='Night Court' Renewed For Season 2 At NBC|url=https://deadline.com/2023/02/night-court-renewal-season-2-nbc-1235248275/|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|first=Rosy|last=Cordero|date=February 2, 2023|access-date=February 9, 2023}}</ref> A third season of the revival series would then premiere on November 19, 2024.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://collider.com/night-court-wendie-malick-season-3-juliannes-background/|title='Night Court's Wendie Malick Teases Season 3 Will Explore Julianne's Background|first=Lade|last=Omotade|publisher=Collider|date=November 20, 2024|accessdate=November 20, 2024}}</ref> On May 9, 2025, the series has been canceled after three seasons.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://variety.com/2025/tv/news/night-court-canceled-nbc-1236392394/|title='Night Court' Canceled After Three Seasons at NBC|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|first=Joe|last=Otterson|date=May 9, 2025|access-date=May 9, 2025}}</ref>

